        "original_id": "962d1c17-4924-583e-aea7-0df0c5748fb4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Rensselaer Plateau, Adirondack Mountains, Round Pond, Berlin, New York, eastern Quebec, Nova Scotia, New England, Appalachians, western North Carolina\n- Species: Picea rubens, red spruce, Eastern White Pine, Eastern Hemlock, Balsam Fir, Red Pine, Tamarack\n- Topics: Vegetation, geologic formations, native range\n\nFacts:\n- The Rensselaer Plateau has vegetation more similar to the Adirondack Mountains than to surrounding areas.\n- The Rensselaer Plateau has abundant Eastern White Pine, Eastern Hemlock, Red Spruce, and Balsam Fir, with more limited occurrences of Red Pine and Tamarack.\n- Picea rubens, commonly known as red spruce, is a spruce species native to eastern North America.\n- Red spruce ranges from eastern Quebec to Nova Scotia and from New England south through the Adirondack Mountains and Appalachians to western North Carolina.\n- Round Pond in Berlin, New York, is located on the geologic formation known as the Rensselaer Plateau.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
